From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Seal, the administrator prompt

Replaces the polkit "Authentication Required" dialog with Seal: a
centred card over a blurred scrim covering every monitor, with the
requesting app's icon and a lock badge (or a neutral system lock when the
requester cannot be resolved), a title and body written by Luma, one
field that is visible and focused from the first frame, a status line,
equal Cancel and Allow buttons and a Details disclosure. Quick Options'
tokens in light, dark, frost and glass; reduced motion drops every
transform, the breathing reader glyph and the shake.

A password is always offered; a fingerprint is an addition.

Mechanism (lumaSealAuth.js). Luma's PAM stack (authselect local
with-fingerprint) runs `auth sufficient pam_fprintd.so` before
`auth sufficient pam_unix.so` in the polkit-1 service, so one polkit
session asks for nothing but a finger until the reader times out. The
stack is not changed. Seal runs more than one PolkitAgent.Session for the
same cookie, as GDM runs a fingerprint conversation beside the password
one:

- The first session claims the reader through pam_fprintd and listens.
- When a password is submitted while that session holds the reader, a
  second session starts. fprintd refuses it the claim (AlreadyInUse),
  pam_fprintd returns PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL without a message, `sufficient`
  ignores that, and pam_unix asks the second session for the password at
  once. The typed text is handed over; nobody waits for the reader to time
  out and nothing is retyped.
- Whichever session first asks for a secret gets the submitted text,
  exactly once; it is not kept afterwards.
- The first session to complete authorized wins. Every other session is
  disconnected before it is cancelled, so a fingerprint message still on
  its way (the failure mode 0085 fixed for unlocking) can neither hold nor
  undo the success. polkit is answered before the card's closing motion.
- Without a reader or an enrolled finger pam_fprintd returns at once and
  the first session asks for the password; nothing about fingerprints is
  shown. After a reader timeout the reader is armed again (at most four
  times); after pam_fprintd's own max-tries it is not.
- The shell decides nothing: polkitd accepts an authorization only from
  its agent helper, for this cookie and for an offered identity.

Copy (lumaSealCopy.js). polkitd passes agents only the action id, the
mechanism's translated message and the subject and caller pids; the
mechanism's other details are withheld. Templates for udisks2 open-device
for writing and for reading (drive name from udisks' own message, size and
partition from sysfs), Flatpak app-install, timedate1 set-time and pkexec
as root (command from pkexec's argument vector); anything else is "App
wants administrator access" with polkit's message as the body. The
requester is resolved from the kernel's view of polkit.subject-pid and its
parents: /.flatpak-info, the systemd unit's application id, or the app
owning a window; otherwise "An app" and the neutral lock. The requesting
app cannot choose the words or the icon.
ShellPolkitAuthenticationAgent gains get_request_detail() so the prompt
can read the pids polkitd passes.

Dropping this patch restores GNOME's polkit dialog.
